<Article
  eyebrow="Homelab · Build log"
  title="Weather apps suck, so I built my own"
  description="A weather page for my own hill, with the public forecast models calibrated against my station's history. The bugs were the best part."
  date="September 2026"
  readingTime="8 min read"
>
  <h2>The forecast is for somewhere else</h2>

  <p>Open any weather app at my house and it gives me the nearest station. That
  station is six miles away and about 700 feet downhill.</p>

  <p>The difference matters. My hill is windier than the valley, colder at night,
  and fogs in when the valley is clear. The app answers a question about a
  different place and presents the answer as though it were about mine.</p>

  <p>I already had an Ambient Weather station on the roof, so I had the
  observations. What I lacked was a forecast that knew anything about my hill,
  and that second problem is the interesting one.</p>

  <h2>What it is</h2>

  <p>One Python script, standard library only, renders a static HTML page every
  sixty seconds. A tiny HTTP server serves it and a loop runs it. There is no
  database, no framework and no build step.</p>

  <p>It runs in a <code>python:3.12-slim</code> container on my Unraid server,
  and the container barely earns its keep: two files and a <code>while true</code>
  loop. The page has four views (Current, Radar, Detailed, Discussion), five
  themes, and installs to a phone home screen as a web app.</p>

  <figure>
    <div class="shots">
      {themes.map((t) => (
        <img src={`/homelab/weather/current-${t}.png`} alt={`The Current view in the ${t} theme`}
             width="860" height="1864" loading="lazy" />
      ))}
    </div>
    <figcaption>The Current view in all five themes: light, dark, retro, paper and
    minimal. These are from the demo station the public version ships with.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>The themes are more fun than they need to be. There is a light one and a dark
  one, obviously. There is one modeled on the Weather Channel's WeatherStar 4000,
  with banded blue panels, hard black text shadows and cartoon icons, because if
  you build your own weather page and skip the WeatherStar theme, what are you even
  doing. There is a graph-paper one that matches this website, and a gray minimal
  one for e-ink screens.</p>

  <p>Adding a theme turned out to require registering it in four separate places,
  and only three of them fail loudly. The fourth is a small script that runs before
  the stylesheet and checks the saved theme against a hardcoded list of names.
  Forget it and the page paints light, then snaps to your theme on every load.</p>

  <h2>The part I care about</h2>

  <p>Anyone can draw a thermometer on a web page. The part worth building was
  calibrating the public forecast models against my own station's history.</p>

  <p>For every day the station has recorded, the page fetches the archived model
  forecast for that exact point and compares it with what the station measured.
  The ratio between the two is the correction. Do that per month and per wind
  direction and you get a table that describes your site.</p>

  <p>Mine has the trees in it.</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>Forecast to gauge wind factor</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>March (bare)</td><td>about 0.30</td></tr>
      <tr><td>July (leaf-on)</td><td>about 0.15</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>The model says twelve mph and the hill delivers two. That gap is canopy, and
  it swings by a factor of two between March and July. A general-purpose weather
  app can't know that about your yard, because it has never seen your yard's
  history.</p>

  <p>Other things the same method turned up:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Gusts need one flat factor.</strong> Mine is about 0.49 and barely
    moves by season or direction. Sustained wind gets taxed by the trees; gusts are
    momentum punched down from above and mostly ignore them.</li>
    <li><strong>Pick your precipitation model with evidence.</strong> Summed against
    my gauge over the whole record, ECMWF came in at 86.4% and GFS at 53.5%. GFS
    reads about half the rain that actually falls here. The page uses ECMWF alone
    for rain amounts, and I have the numbers for why.</li>
    <li><strong>"Windy" should be relative to your own site.</strong> Scoring daily
    wind run against the station's own percentile distribution turns an abstract
    number into <code>30 mi · 81st percentile · breezy</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The bugs are the good part</h2>

  <p>Three things were wrong for months without looking wrong. That is the real
  lesson of the project, and it is why I now distrust any dashboard I have not
  tried to falsify.</p>

  <p><strong>Rain read 0.00 every single day.</strong> The code took the last
  record of the day and read its running total. The API returns records newest
  first, so "last" was 00:05, five minutes after the daily counter resets. The
  thirty-day rain chart had shown zero for its entire existence. Fixed, the same
  window showed fifteen wet days and 5.00 inches.</p>

  <p><strong>The station's own history file stopped growing</strong> in July, and
  nothing noticed. The code that should have appended to it was already fetching
  the data and throwing the fields away. It sat frozen at 101 days. It is now at
  131 and maintains itself at no extra API cost.</p>

  <p><strong>Every single day was classified a "rager."</strong> Wind run was
  computed as <code>sum / 4.0</code>, which assumes fifteen-minute archive records.
  Ambient logs every five minutes. Every day scored triple against a percentile
  scale that topped out at 49.7 miles, so nearly every day came back 99th
  percentile. The same conditions now read <code>30 mi · 81st · breezy</code>.</p>

  <p>None of these threw an error. All three rendered a confident number in a nice
  font.</p>

  <h3>A few that were just funny</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>The moon never drew, in any theme, ever.</strong> The crescent was
    two arcs, and the return arc's radius was smaller than half its own chord. The
    SVG spec scales an out-of-range radius up until it fits, so both arcs resolved
    to the same semicircle on the same center with opposite sweeps. The path
    enclosed zero area and nothing painted. Every clear night showed no icon. My
    first fix blamed the fill color, and the dark theme disproved that in one
    screenshot.</li>
    <li><strong>The moon phase also ran 1.5 days behind</strong> for a while. The
    ephemeris formulas count days from 1999-12-31, and I had assumed the J2000 noon
    epoch like everyone else.</li>
    <li><strong>Today's high vanished after sunset.</strong> NWS stops publishing a
    daytime period once the day is over, and the fallback took the maximum of a
    one-element list, which is just the current temperature. The page cheerfully
    reported <code>H:47° L:45°</code> on a day that reached 68. After dark, today's
    high is a measurement, so it now comes from the station's own archive.</li>
    <li><strong>The wind chart was unreadable</strong> for a structural reason I had
    to be told about. It plotted speed in mph and direction in degrees on one
    dual-axis chart. Direction is circular, so a northerly wind alternated between 0
    and 360 and painted dots along the top and bottom at once. Direction now has its
    own strip of arrows below the plot, and arrows handle north fine.</li>
    <li><strong><code>hourlyrainin</code> is a trap.</strong> It reads like "rain
    this hour" and is a rolling sixty-minute total, so hourly bars built from it
    count the same rain twelve times.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The traps nobody writes down</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>NWS rejects coordinates with more than four decimals.</strong> It
    answers with an HTML error page instead of JSON.</li>
    <li><strong>NWS requires a User-Agent header with a real contact.</strong>
    Without one you get a 403.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify your coordinates before calibrating anything.</strong> An
    early build ran for two days on a point six kilometers off, and every
    calibration constant had to be recomputed.</li>
    <li><strong>Ambient's API allows one request per second.</strong> Sleep between
    backfill pages.</li>
    <li><strong>On a Mac, launchd cannot read <code>~/Documents</code>,
    <code>~/Desktop</code> or <code>~/Downloads</code>.</strong> A job that works in
    Terminal and dies under launchd with "Operation not permitted" is this, every
    time.</li>
    <li><strong>A chart built inside a hidden container sizes itself to
    zero.</strong> Every lazy-loaded tab has to build its charts the first time it
    is shown.</li>
    <li><strong>Serve only the output directory.</strong> The config file with the
    API keys sits beside the renderer, outside anything the web server can
    reach.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>That last one deserves more than a bullet. I put the page behind Basic Auth
  and only checked that the auth was enforced sixteen days later. It had been open
  to the internet the whole time, on a page that pins my house on a map. Nothing
  came of it. It was still sixteen days of assuming instead of checking, the same
  mistake as the rain bug in different clothes.</p>

  <h2>Run it on your own hill</h2>

  <figure>
    <img src="/homelab/weather/desktop-light.png" alt="The Current view on a desktop browser, light theme"
         width="2560" height="1720" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>The public version on a desktop browser, pointed at its demo
    station.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>The method travels. Calibrate the public models against whatever your own
  sensor has recorded and let the difference describe your site.</p>

  <p>The code is on GitHub in <a href={repo}>homelab-builds/tradweather</a>.
  Everything site-specific lives in one <code>config.json</code>: coordinates,
  elevation, station name, timezone, your API keys, and your contact address for
  the NWS header. Clone it, fill in the config, run the container.</p>

  <p>Two caveats:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>The NWS half is United States only.</strong> Forecasts, alerts, the
    forecast discussion and NEXRAD radar all come from NOAA. Outside the US the
    station panels and the Open-Meteo charts still work, and the NWS sections render
    empty.</li>
    <li><strong>Calibration needs history.</strong> It improves on its own as your
    record grows and is useful after a season. My own record is still spring and
    summer only, so eight of my twelve monthly wind factors are still fallback
    values. Ask me again in March.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Credit</h2>

  <p>The original is Porter Haney's Hilltop House dashboard: the renderer, the
  calibration method, and the whole idea of scoring models against your own gauge.
  Mine is that work re-pointed at a different vendor's API and a different hill,
  published with his blessing.</p>
</Article>
